###Pure
// custody-recon -- WHAT THE CUSTODIAN SAYS WE HOLD, PUT AGAINST WHAT WE SAY WE OWN.
//
// Layer 2. Depends on custody-core (the holdings) and position-keeping (the positions),
// and on nothing else. It does not reach past them to core-account or core-instrument:
// everything it needs about an account or an instrument it reaches THROUGH a holding or
// a position.
//
// THE PROBLEM THIS PROJECT EXISTS FOR. The two sides are not keyed the same way and no
// amount of goodwill makes them line up:
//
//     a custody holding is   (institution, account, instrument, DEPOT)
//     an internal position is (institution, account, instrument, DATE)
//
// Three of the four columns agree. The fourth does not, and it does not disagree
// symmetrically: DEPOT is a place and DATE is a moment. Custody has no opinion about
// dates -- a holding is a standing statement of where stock sits -- and position keeping
// has no opinion about places -- a position is what an account owns, wherever it happens
// to be sitting. So the join between them is three columns wide plus a date supplied by
// the RUN, and it is one-to-MANY in the depot direction: the same position faces every
// depot the account keeps that instrument in, and there is normally more than one.
//
// TWO CONSTRUCTS CARRY THAT.
//
//   COMPOSITE KEY  Nothing here is identified by fewer than two columns and the spine is
//                  identified by six. A ReconItem is
//                  (reconCode, cobDate, institution, account, instrument, depot) --
//                  the union of both key shapes, which is the only key that can hold one
//                  comparison, because dropping the depot merges two custodians' answers
//                  and dropping the date merges two days' runs. A ReconBreak adds a
//                  seventh column, its type.
//
//   AGGREGATION    Four classes are per-group TOTALS on store Views, and one of them is
//                  the reconciliation itself: DepotRollup groups the items by the FIVE
//                  columns a position has, which is to say it groups AWAY the depot. That
//                  GROUP BY is the whole trick -- it is what converts a set of
//                  holding-shaped rows into one position-shaped row that a position can
//                  actually be compared against. The other three totals are the
//                  management view: per account, per break type, per assignee.
//
// A run, its matched items, its breaks by type, their ageing, their assignment and their
// resolution are all here, because a reconciliation that only tells you the difference
// and not who is fixing it is a report, not a control.

// -------------------------------------------------------------- the standing set-up

// THE STANDING DEFINITION OF ONE RECONCILIATION -- which two sources are compared, how
// often, and who owns the answer. Every other row in the project is stamped with its
// reconCode, so two reconciliations of the same holdings (an intraday one and the
// official overnight one) never contaminate each other's breaks.
Class custody_recon::ReconDefinition
{
   reconCode: String[1];

   reconName: String[1];
   // Named rather than typed, because the pair is the point: custody is source A and the
   // internal book is source B, and which one is "right" is decided break by break.
   sourceASystem: String[1];
   sourceBSystem: String[1];
   frequency: String[1];
   ownerTeam: String[1];
   defaultToleranceQuantity: Float[0..1];
   introducedOn: StrictDate[1];
   retiredOn: StrictDate[0..1];

   toleranceRules: custody_recon::ToleranceRule[*];
   ageingBands: custody_recon::AgeingBand[*];
   runs: custody_recon::ReconRun[*];

   // A null retirement date is the only honest test of liveness -- a Filter will not take
   // a boolean literal either, so the store agrees with the model here.
   isLive() { $this.retiredOn->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// HOW SMALL A DIFFERENCE COUNTS AS ZERO, for one scope of one reconciliation. Keyed by
// THREE columns -- (reconCode, ruleScope, ruleCode) -- because a rule code such as
// `BOND-ROUNDING` means one thing under the scope `INSTRUMENT_CLASS` and another under
// `MARKET`, and both live under their own reconciliation.
Class custody_recon::ToleranceRule
{
   reconCode: String[1];
   ruleScope: String[1];
   ruleCode: String[1];

   toleranceQuantity: Float[0..1];
   tolerancePercent: Float[0..1];
   toleranceAmount: Float[0..1];
   toleranceCurrency: String[0..1];
   appliesFrom: StrictDate[1];
   appliesTo: StrictDate[0..1];
   ruleNote: String[0..1];

   definition: custody_recon::ReconDefinition[1];
   items: custody_recon::ReconItem[*];

   ruleKey() { $this.reconCode + '/' + $this.ruleScope + '/' + $this.ruleCode } : String[1];
   isCurrent() { $this.appliesTo->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// ONE NAMED BUCKET OF BREAK AGE -- `0-1D`, `2-5D`, `6-30D`, `31D+` -- and what it
// escalates to. Ageing is banded rather than left as a raw day count because escalation
// is a step function: nobody escalates on day four and a half.
Class custody_recon::AgeingBand
{
   reconCode: String[1];
   bandCode: String[1];

   bandLabel: String[1];
   minAgeDays: Integer[1];
   // Absent on the top band. That band is where breaks go to be argued about.
   maxAgeDays: Integer[0..1];
   escalationLevel: String[1];
   bandOrder: Integer[1];

   definition: custody_recon::ReconDefinition[1];
   breaks: custody_recon::ReconBreak[*];

   isOpenEnded() { $this.maxAgeDays->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------- the run and its output

// ONE EXECUTION OF ONE RECONCILIATION FOR ONE DAY. Keyed by THREE columns --
// (reconCode, cobDate, runSeq) -- because a day is reconciled more than once: the first
// run at 06:00 against an overnight statement, then again after the late statement
// arrives, and the second run must not overwrite the evidence of the first.
//
// The run is also where the DATE that the two key shapes are missing comes from. Custody
// holdings carry no date of their own; the run's cobDate is what pins them to a position.
Class custody_recon::ReconRun
{
   reconCode: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   runSeq: Integer[1];

   runType: String[1];
   runStatus: String[1];
   startedOn: StrictDate[1];
   completedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   lineCount: Integer[1];
   itemCount: Integer[1];
   matchedCount: Integer[1];
   breakCount: Integer[1];
   triggeredBy: String[1];

   definition: custody_recon::ReconDefinition[1];
   lines: custody_recon::ReconLine[*];
   items: custody_recon::ReconItem[*];

   isComplete() { $this.completedOn->isEmpty()->not() } : Boolean[1];

   // Integer / Integer widens to Float on its own, so this is Float and not Integer.
   matchRate() { $this.matchedCount / $this.itemCount } : Float[1];
}

// ONE INTERNAL POSITION PUT UP AGAINST EVERYTHING CUSTODY SAYS ABOUT IT. This is the
// POSITION-SHAPED side of the reconciliation: keyed by FIVE columns --
// (reconCode, cobDate, institutionId, accountNo, instrumentId) -- which is exactly
// position-keeping's four-column position key with the reconciliation's code in front of
// it. There is deliberately no depot here; that is what makes it comparable to a
// position at all.
//
// Its custodyQuantity is the SUM over the depots underneath it. One line, several items.
Class custody_recon::ReconLine
{
   reconCode: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   instrumentId: String[1];

   runSeq: Integer[1];
   // How many depots this one position had to be spread over. A line with depotCount 1 is
   // the easy case and most lines are not it.
   depotCount: Integer[1];
   custodyQuantity: Float[1];
   internalQuantity: Float[1];
   differenceQuantity: Float[1];
   differenceValue: Float[0..1];
   priceCurrency: String[0..1];
   quantityUnit: String[1];
   lineStatus: String[1];
   isWithinTolerance: Boolean[1];
   matchedOn: StrictDate[0..1];

   run: custody_recon::ReconRun[1];
   items: custody_recon::ReconItem[*];

   // CROSS-PROJECT, and the reason this class is shaped the way it is: three shared
   // columns plus the run's cobDate reach exactly one position.
   position: position_keeping::Position[0..1];
   // Movements totalled for the same position-day. A quantity break that equals the day's
   // net movement is a timing break wearing a disguise, and this is what shows it.
   movementTotal: position_keeping::MovementTotal[0..1];
   ladder: position_keeping::SettlementLadderEntry[*];

   // The depot-grain items collapsed back to this line's own shape. Reading it does not
   // read the item rows.
   rollup: custody_recon::DepotRollup[0..1];
   accountTotal: custody_recon::AccountReconTotal[0..1];

   lineKey() { $this.reconCode + '/' + $this.institutionId + '/' + $this.accountNo + '/' + $this.instrumentId } : String[1];

   // Recomputed rather than read from differenceQuantity, on purpose: if the two disagree
   // the stored figure is from an earlier run and the line is stale.
   custodyLessInternal() { $this.custodyQuantity - $this.internalQuantity } : Float[1];

   isMatched() { $this.matchedOn->isEmpty()->not() } : Boolean[1];
   facesSeveralDepots() { $this.depotCount > 1 } : Boolean[1];
}

// ONE COMPARISON AT THE DEPOT GRAIN -- the spine of the project and the class that holds
// BOTH key shapes at once. SIX key columns:
//
//     reconCode + cobDate + institutionId + accountNo + instrumentId + depotCode
//                 \___ position-keeping's fourth ___/               \_ custody-core's _/
//
// Take the four middle-and-right columns without cobDate and you have custody-core's
// holding key exactly; take them without depotCode and you have position-keeping's
// position key exactly. Neither key is a prefix of the other, which is why the item has
// to carry both and why no shorter key would do: drop depotCode and two custodians'
// answers merge into one row, drop cobDate and yesterday's run overwrites today's.
//
// The internalQuantity on an item is the position's quantity ALLOCATED to this depot,
// and allocationBasis records how -- a position cannot be split by any rule custody
// knows about, so the split is a stated policy rather than a fact.
Class custody_recon::ReconItem
{
   reconCode: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   instrumentId: String[1];
   depotCode: String[1];

   runSeq: Integer[1];

   // Custody's answer, from the holding. Zero when there is no holding at all.
   custodyQuantity: Float[1];
   custodySettledQuantity: Float[1];
   // Our answer, or this depot's share of it.
   internalQuantity: Float[1];
   internalSettledQuantity: Float[1];
   differenceQuantity: Float[1];

   // SINGLE_DEPOT, PRIMARY_DEPOT, PRO_RATA or UNALLOCATED. UNALLOCATED is the honest
   // answer when an account holds one instrument at three depots and nothing in the
   // internal book says which depot owns which share -- the line still reconciles, the
   // item does not.
   allocationBasis: String[1];
   allocationRatio: Float[0..1];

   matchStatus: String[1];
   // Which side actually turned up. Both false cannot happen; either false is a break.
   hasCustodySide: Boolean[1];
   hasInternalSide: Boolean[1];
   toleranceScope: String[0..1];
   toleranceCode: String[0..1];
   matchedOn: StrictDate[0..1];

   line: custody_recon::ReconLine[1];
   run: custody_recon::ReconRun[1];
   breaks: custody_recon::ReconBreak[*];
   toleranceRule: custody_recon::ToleranceRule[0..1];
   rollup: custody_recon::DepotRollup[0..1];

   // CROSS-PROJECT, custody side. Four columns, no date -- a holding has none.
   holding: custody_core::CustodyHolding[0..1];
   depot: custody_core::Depot[0..1];
   // In-flight receipts and deliveries at this depot: the usual explanation of a timing
   // break, and the reason the depot column cannot be aggregated away before matching.
   pendingMovements: custody_core::PendingMovement[*];
   // The custodian's own dated figure. This join is where the two shapes meet head-on:
   // four holding columns AND the run's cobDate against the statement's own date.
   custodianStatement: custody_core::CustodyStatementLine[0..1];

   // CROSS-PROJECT, internal side. Three shared columns plus cobDate, and DEPOT_CODE is
   // deliberately not in it -- which is precisely why several items point at one position.
   position: position_keeping::Position[0..1];

   // The custody-shaped identity of this comparison: what custody-core calls this row.
   holdingKey() { $this.institutionId + '/' + $this.accountNo + '/' + $this.instrumentId + '/' + $this.depotCode } : String[1];

   // The position-shaped identity of the same comparison, minus its date. Two derived
   // properties on one class because the row genuinely has two names.
   positionKey() { $this.institutionId + '/' + $this.accountNo + '/' + $this.instrumentId } : String[1];

   signedDifference() { $this.custodyQuantity - $this.internalQuantity } : Float[1];
   isMatched() { $this.matchedOn->isEmpty()->not() } : Boolean[1];

   // Custody has it and we do not: stock at a depot nobody booked. The dangerous one.
   isCustodyOnly() { $this.hasInternalSide->not() } : Boolean[1];
   // We have it and custody does not: a position with nowhere to sit.
   isInternalOnly() { $this.hasCustodySide->not() } : Boolean[1];

   // The split was a guess. A break on an unallocated item is a modelling problem, not an
   // operational one, and is triaged differently.
   isUnallocated() { $this.allocationBasis == 'UNALLOCATED' } : Boolean[1];
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------- breaks and their life

// ONE DISAGREEMENT OF ONE TYPE ON ONE ITEM. SEVEN key columns: the item's six plus
// breakType, because a single item can fail in more than one way at once -- a holding
// can be short by 500 AND have an unmatched receipt in flight -- and the two are worked
// by different people.
//
// The four types are the four things that can be wrong:
//   QUANTITY       both sides are there and the numbers differ by more than tolerance
//   MISSING_HERE   custody has a holding; the internal book has no position
//   MISSING_THERE  the internal book has a position; custody has no holding at that depot
//   TIMING         the difference is exactly explained by something in flight and will
//                  clear itself on settlement date
//
// breakRef is a mint-once reference for the workflow layer. It exists because assignment
// and resolution are done by humans through tickets, and a seven-column natural key is
// not something anybody pastes into one.
Class custody_recon::ReconBreak
{
   reconCode: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   instrumentId: String[1];
   depotCode: String[1];
   breakType: String[1];

   breakRef: String[1];
   breakQuantity: Float[1];
   breakValue: Float[0..1];
   breakCurrency: String[0..1];
   severity: String[1];

   // Ageing. detectedOn is this run's; firstSeenOn is the first run that ever saw this
   // break, and it survives the break being re-detected every night. Ageing off
   // detectedOn would reset the clock daily, which is how a six-month break gets reported
   // as one day old.
   detectedOn: StrictDate[1];
   firstSeenOn: StrictDate[1];
   ageDays: Integer[1];
   ageingBandCode: String[0..1];

   assignedTo: String[0..1];
   assignedTeam: String[0..1];
   breakStatus: String[1];
   resolvedOn: StrictDate[0..1];

   item: custody_recon::ReconItem[1];
   ageingBand: custody_recon::AgeingBand[0..1];
   assignments: custody_recon::BreakAssignment[*];
   resolution: custody_recon::BreakResolution[0..1];
   typeTotal: custody_recon::BreakTypeTotal[0..1];
   assigneeLoad: custody_recon::AssigneeLoad[0..1];

   isOpen() { $this.resolvedOn->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   isQuantityBreak() { $this.breakType == 'QUANTITY' } : Boolean[1];
   isTimingBreak() { $this.breakType == 'TIMING' } : Boolean[1];
   // Either side missing entirely. Read together, these two are the whole "missing here /
   // missing there" pair.
   isOneSided() { $this.breakType == 'MISSING_HERE' || $this.breakType == 'MISSING_THERE' } : Boolean[1];
   isUnassigned() { $this.assignedTo->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// ONE HANDOVER OF ONE BREAK TO ONE PERSON. Keyed by (reconCode, breakRef, assignmentSeq)
// -- THREE columns -- because a break is reassigned: ops picks it up, hands it to the
// custody team, who hand it back. Kept as a sequence of rows rather than one mutable
// owner field so the trail is still readable at audit.
Class custody_recon::BreakAssignment
{
   reconCode: String[1];
   breakRef: String[1];
   assignmentSeq: Integer[1];

   assignedTo: String[1];
   assignedTeam: String[1];
   assignedOn: StrictDate[1];
   assignedBy: String[1];
   dueOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   releasedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   assignmentNote: String[0..1];

   reconBreak: custody_recon::ReconBreak[1];

   isCurrent() { $this.releasedOn->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// HOW A BREAK WAS CLOSED, and by whose authority. One per break, keyed by
// (reconCode, breakRef). correctionSide is the part that matters months later: whether
// custody was corrected, the internal book was corrected, or neither was and the break
// was written off.
Class custody_recon::BreakResolution
{
   reconCode: String[1];
   breakRef: String[1];

   resolutionType: String[1];
   resolvedOn: StrictDate[1];
   resolvedBy: String[1];
   rootCause: String[1];
   correctionSide: String[1];
   correctionQuantity: Float[0..1];
   resolutionNote: String[0..1];
   approvedBy: String[0..1];

   reconBreak: custody_recon::ReconBreak[1];

   // A write-off that nobody signed off is an open item wearing a closed status.
   isApproved() { $this.approvedBy->isEmpty()->not() } : Boolean[1];
   isWriteOff() { $this.resolutionType == 'WRITE_OFF' } : Boolean[1];
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------- the aggregations

// THE RECONCILIATION OF THE TWO KEY SHAPES, EXPRESSED AS A GROUP BY. One row is one
// (reconCode, cobDate, institution, account, instrument) -- the position's shape -- and
// it is built by grouping the depot-grain items and DROPPING the depot column. That is
// the whole answer to "how do you compare a four-column holding key with a different
// four-column position key": you do not compare them, you aggregate one of them until it
// has the other's shape.
//
// It is a store View, not a table: no DDL, nothing seeds it, and the engine folds the
// GROUP BY into its SQL. Navigating here from a line crosses into a group and never into
// another item row. Note what a grouping does not produce: a position with no custody
// items at all forms NO group, and is absent rather than showing a total of zero -- which
// is exactly why MISSING_THERE has to be detected on the items and cannot be read off
// this view.
Class custody_recon::DepotRollup
{
   reconCode: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   instrumentId: String[1];

   depotCount: Integer[1];
   totalCustodyQuantity: Float[1];
   totalInternalQuantity: Float[1];
   netDifference: Float[1];
   largestDepotQuantity: Float[1];

   line: custody_recon::ReconLine[0..1];
   items: custody_recon::ReconItem[*];
   // The group faces the position directly, on the same three columns plus the date. This
   // is the join the whole project is built to make possible.
   position: position_keeping::Position[0..1];

   differenceAgainstPosition() { $this.totalCustodyQuantity - $this.totalInternalQuantity } : Float[1];

   // Float / Integer does not type as Float, so the count is widened first.
   averageDepotQuantity() { $this.totalCustodyQuantity / $this.depotCount->toFloat() } : Float[1];

   facesSeveralDepots() { $this.depotCount > 1 } : Boolean[1];
}

// ONE ACCOUNT'S WHOLE RECONCILIATION ON ONE DAY, grouped out of the lines. What a client
// service desk opens in the morning: how many instruments were compared for this account,
// how much of it disagrees, and by how much at worst. The grouping drops the instrument.
Class custody_recon::AccountReconTotal
{
   reconCode: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];

   lineCount: Integer[1];
   totalCustodyQuantity: Float[1];
   totalInternalQuantity: Float[1];
   totalDifferenceQuantity: Float[1];
   totalDifferenceValue: Float[0..1];
   largestDifference: Float[1];

   lines: custody_recon::ReconLine[*];

   averageDifference() { $this.totalDifferenceQuantity / $this.lineCount->toFloat() } : Float[1];
}

// BREAKS OF ONE TYPE ON ONE DAY, TOTALLED -- the count, the money and the oldest one.
// The grouping is (reconCode, cobDate, breakType), which is the shape of the daily break
// report: four rows, one per type, and the ops manager reads the OLDEST_AGE_DAYS column
// first because a growing maximum age is worse news than a growing count.
Class custody_recon::BreakTypeTotal
{
   reconCode: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   breakType: String[1];

   breakCount: Integer[1];
   totalBreakQuantity: Float[1];
   totalBreakValue: Float[0..1];
   oldestAgeDays: Integer[1];
   earliestDetectedOn: StrictDate[1];

   breaks: custody_recon::ReconBreak[*];

   averageBreakQuantity() { $this.totalBreakQuantity / $this.breakCount->toFloat() } : Float[1];
}

// ONE PERSON'S BREAK QUEUE across every day of one reconciliation. The grouping drops the
// cob date entirely, which is the point: a break does not stop being yours because the
// date rolled. This is the aggregate that makes ageing actionable rather than merely
// reportable.
Class custody_recon::AssigneeLoad
{
   reconCode: String[1];
   assignedTo: String[1];

   assignedBreakCount: Integer[1];
   totalBreakValue: Float[0..1];
   oldestAgeDays: Integer[1];
   latestCobDate: StrictDate[1];

   breaks: custody_recon::ReconBreak[*];

   isOverloaded() { $this.assignedBreakCount > 25 } : Boolean[1];
}
